      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine how many distinct sequences of four letters can be made from the letters in \"EQUALS\" while ensuring the sequence begins with \"L\" and ends with \"Q\" and no letter appears more than once. Let's break it down step by step:\n\n1. **Identify the fixed positions**: The sequence begins with \"L\" and ends with \"Q\". This leaves us with two more positions to fill with the remaining three letters \"E\", \"U\", and \"S\".\n\n2. **Choose the order of the remaining letters**: We need to choose 2 out of the 3 remaining letters to fill the middle two positions. The order in which we place these letters matters because the sequence is considered in that order.\n\n3. **Calculate the number of ways to choose and arrange the letters**:\n   - We can choose 2 out of the 3 letters (E, U, S) in \\( \\binom{3}{2} \\) ways.\n   - After choosing the 2 letters, we can arrange them in \\(2!\\) (2 factorial) ways.\n\nThe calculation is as follows:\n\\[\n\\text{Number of ways} = \\binom{3}{2} \\times 2! = 3 \\times 2 = 6\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct sequences of four letters that can be made from \"EQUALS\" where the sequence begins with \"L\", ends with \"Q\", and no letter appears more than once is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
